TypeScript allows us to specify the "discriminated union type" which indicates that a value could belong to multiple types, but the type it will ultimately have is identified by a specific property (called the discriminator). This property, when evaluated, can determine the exact type of the value and provide type safety.

For example, you can create a function that accepts an input of type `{id: number, type: 'a' | 'b' | 'c'}`, which can belonging to a union type until `type` is evaluated:
```typescript
function processInput({ id, type }: { id: number; type: 'a' | 'b' | 'c' }) {
  switch (type) {
    case 'a':
      console.log(`Processing type a with id ${id}`);
      break;
    case 'b':
      console.log(`Processing type b with id ${id}`);
      break;
    case 'c':
      console.log(`Processing type c with id ${id}`);
      break;
  }
}
```
In this case, TypeScript knows that the `type` can be `a`, `b`, or `c`. The actual type is determined by evaluating the `type` and assigning the appropriate logic to each case. This way, if someone passes an input of type `{ id: number, type: 'x' }`, the compiler will provide an error because it cannot map to one of the cases.

So, to use a discriminated union type, you would need to create a union type with a shared property which distinguishes each possible type, and then create a type guard function to determine the actual type when needed.

