Here's a C++ implementation of a function that finds common elements between two given arrays without using any extra data structures:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Finds and returns a pointer to a common element 
// in two sorted arrays ar1[] and ar2[] of sizes n and m 
bool commonElement(int* ar1, int ar1_size, int* ar2, int ar2_size, int *common_element) {
    // if any of the arrays has 0 elements then return false 
    if (ar1_size == 0 || ar2_size == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // Initialize pointers for both arrays 
    int *ptr1 = ar1; 
    int *ptr2 = ar2; 

    while (true) {
        // if both pointers are out of bounds of their arrays then return false 
        if (ptr1 >= ar1 + ar1_size || ptr2 >= ar2 + ar2_size) {
            return false;
        }

        if (*ptr1 == *ptr2) {
            // found a common element between the two arrays 
            *common_element = *ptr1;
            return true;
        }

        // move the pointers such that the next common element can be found 
        // as we are dealing with sorted arrays so the common elements should be 
        // in a row ordered fashion 
        if (*ptr1 < *ptr2) {
            ++ptr1;
        } else {
            ++ptr2;
        }
    }
}

// Driver code 
int main() {
    int ar1[] = { 1, 3, 6, 7, 9, 13 };
    int ar2[] = { 1, 2, 7, 11, 13, 15 };
    int common_element;

    if (commonElement(ar1, 6, ar2, 6, &common_element)) {
        std::cout << "Common Element: " << common_element << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "No Common Element" << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Explanation:
The function `commonElement` takes two arrays as input, along with their lengths. It checks if any of the arrays has 0 elements, which means there are no common elements; in that case, it returns false. Otherwise, it begins a traversal of the arrays using two pointers, one for each array (`ptr1` and `ptr2`). It compares the values pointed by the pointers with each other. If they are equal, it stores the value in `common_element`. If none of the values are equal, it increments either `ptr1` or `ptr2` based on their values.

Assuming your input arrays have unique elements, this implementation will give you a solution that has a time complexity of `O(n)`, where `n` is the total number of elements in both arrays.
